#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153071} :activity/id #uuid "79d375df-4e87-5b44-81dc-23c2a98b6b63" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049094 :topic/id #uuid "da0d77f1-bb5a-5589-bc40-e34367567aeb" :topic/math-academy-id 2677} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153027 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153483 :tutorial/content "Throughout this lesson we've made use of **Bézout's identity**, which states that for any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that any integer combination of $a$ and $b$ is also a multiple of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$. In other words, for *any* integers $u'$ and $v'$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,b) \\mid (au'+bv')\n$$\n\nMoreover, the set of integers\n$\\big\\{au'+bv' \\,:\\, u', v' \\in\\mathbb Z \\big \\}$\ncontains *all* the multiples of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n\nWe'll make use of these results in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "9505be6e-ec53-5537-bf81-d4b7b197c384" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9653 :tutorial/title "Final Remarks"} :step/id #uuid "7e080049-7b49-599a-b9dd-9f6cd03fa661" :step/math-academy-id 183906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152163 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151954 :question/id #uuid "df4647c2-73f7-57eb-b013-68d7f170266a" :question/math-academy-id "e-6496"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152768}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime"} :step/id #uuid "a1c1eb96-adce-53a7-848d-bf42b0fb27f8" :step/math-academy-id 23950 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153027}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153071 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153360 :tutorial/content "For any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nThis is known as **Bézout's identity**.\n\nTo find the integers $u$ and $v$, we can use the **extended Euclidean algorithm**, which involves the following three steps:\n\n1. Carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n2. Solve for the remainder at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder.\n3. Back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $au+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n\nFor example, let's use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n48u+18v= \\textrm{gcd}(48,18)\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$.\n$48|=\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}12; ↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18|=\\end{vmatrix}12 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0$\nSo, we find that $\\text{gcd}(48,18) =6$.\n- **Step 2:** We solve for the remainder (the rightmost term) at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as an integer.\n$12|= 48 - 18 \\cdot 2; 6|= 18 - 12$\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $48u+18v= 6$.\n$6|= 18 - 12; = 18 - (48 - 18 \\cdot 2); = 18 - 48 + 18 \\cdot 2; = (18 + 18 \\cdot 2) - 48; = 18 \\cdot 3 - 48; = 48 \\cdot (-1) + 18 \\cdot (3)$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=3$." :tutorial/id #uuid "6f364b85-4d93-5a09-a174-dea640e1c448" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4024 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0dca946f-169d-57e2-88ac-167fa4ed7275" :step/math-academy-id 24052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153099}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153099 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152184 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151981 :question/id #uuid "ed22bd9f-4dc9-528f-890f-2a81c4d353cd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6763"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4caf9fc6-9247-5807-84de-208b731116e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048141}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152769} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152791}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Not Coprime"} :step/id #uuid "85cccd96-f97c-5a7c-b7a5-e3abb690a42e" :step/math-academy-id 24976 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153065}}] :activity/title "The Extended Euclidean Algorithm" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152344}]}
